  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "72ad68b8-5fe6-4fb1-9431-32c8700c2afd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Residuals are basically the magnitude of the difference between and actual and predicted value, so we are analysing the resiudals of the log prices. My interpretation is that the log prices show less of a difference when the price moves so big movements matter more in this case? When the opposing stock therefore has a big movement it means that it is more meaningful and little movements don't matter as much (it is actually the opposite, the log compresses the changes and makes smaller ones more important as a percentage movement)(essentially the log looks at what power to you would have to have on a base 10 to get to the value input effectively reducing the scale of these closing prices)"
   ]
  },
